---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="Hanfu vs Hanbok: 7 Differences, With Photos"
  description="Hanfu vs hanbok — and hanbok vs hanfu — explained: 7 side-by-side differences in collar, sleeves, jacket length and skirt volume, with photos and history."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.dresses-hanfu.com/comparisons/hanbok/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Comparisons", url: "/comparisons" }, { name: "Hanfu vs Hanbok", url: "/comparisons/hanbok" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-09-14",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/comparisons" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/comparisons/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Comparisons</a> &rsaquo; Hanfu vs Hanbok</p>
          <h1>Hanfu vs Hanbok: Similar Root, Different Result</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            The Korean hanbok and Chinese hanfu share a distant ancestor in ancient East Asian robes — and both cross the collar right over left. But centuries of separate evolution gave them different proportions, different sleeves, and different energy.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">

        <div class="media-card" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem;">
          <div class="media-card-img">
            <img src="/images/pexels-hanfu-woman.jpg" srcset="/images/opt/pexels-hanfu-woman-200w.webp 200w, /images/opt/pexels-hanfu-woman-400w.webp 400w, /images/opt/pexels-hanfu-woman-600w.webp 600w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 440px" alt="Woman in serene traditional Hanfu portrait in soft daylight" width="1400" height="2100" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
          </div>
          <div class="media-card-body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">
            <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Side by Side</p>
            <p style="font-size:1.375rem;font-weight:600;font-family:var(--font-display);margin:0;">Same root, different silhouette.</p>
            <p style="font-size:0.9375rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">Hanbok and hanfu both trace back to Tang-era dress — then parted ways in cut, length and volume.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

          <h2>The Quick Answer</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Hanbok is the traditional dress of Korea. Like much of Korean court culture, proto-hanbok borrowed heavily from Tang-dynasty China, but the silhouette that survives today is distinctly Korean: women's hanbok has a much shorter, cropped jeogori jacket with a high ribbon (aot) tied at the chest, and a long, full, high-waisted chima (skirt). Hanfu has a longer cross-collar top over a skirt, and the collar crosses at a different line.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hanfu</th><th>Hanbok</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Origin</strong></td><td>Han Chinese, ancient</td><td>Korean, influenced by Tang/Song Chinese plus native Korean cuts</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Top (women)</strong></td><td>Ru — knee-or-hip length cross-collared top</td><td>Jeogori — short cropped jacket, mid-chest to mid-rib</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Skirt</strong></td><td>Long wrap skirt, varies</td><td>Voluminous, high-waisted (sometimes above the bust</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Belt</strong></td><td>Sash tied at waist or bust</td><td>aot kkot ribbon at the chest</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Sleeves</strong></td><td>Dynasty-dependent width</td><td>Bat ray sleeves for women with flat thumbs (ot kkotsae)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Collar</strong></td><td>Crosses right over left across chest</td><td>V-shape / cross, shaped differently, wider lapel</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Monthly layering</strong></td><td>Yes, important</td><td>Minimal — typically two pieces total</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Why People Confuse Them</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">The shared ancestor</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Tang dynasty Chinese dress influenced the Three Kingdoms of Korea. Both use the same cross-collar concept and both billow with wide skirts. But by the Koryo and Joseon dynasties, Korea had developed its own look — cropped top, high-ribbon waist, and fuller skirt compared to Hanfu.
          </p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">The proportion tell</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            In photos, the easiest tell is proportion: hanbok looks like volume concentrated in a high, full skirt; Hanfu reads as long vertical lines.
          
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">One Easy Memory: The Top</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Look at the upper body. Korean hanbok: top is short, ribbon sits at the chest, everything below balloons out. Hanfu: top is longer, down to the knees or hip, collar crosses mid-chest, and the waist sits naturally. Short cropped top + high chest ribbon = hanbok. Longer layered top, natural waist = Hanfu.
          </p>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">More Comparisons</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/comparisons/kimono/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu vs Kimono</a>
              <a href="/comparisons/qipao/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu vs Qipao</a>
              <a href="/comparisons/cheongsam/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu vs Cheongsam</a>
              <a href="/comparisons/" class="btn btn-outline">All Comparisons</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>